      "id": "dimpled-ball-2026",
      "category": "Equipment",
      "headline": "Could a dimpled ball change the game?",
      "summary": "Ray D'Alonzo has published a mathematical paper on golf-style dimples on a table tennis ball. His model suggests less drag and more speed kept across the table, though the effects on spin and bounce would need real prototypes to test.",
      "date": "2026-10-23",
      "sources": [
        {
          "name": "Table Tennis England",
          "url": "https://www.tabletennisengland.co.uk/news/2026/a-table-tennis-ball-with-dimples-that-could-be-a-whole-new-ball-game/"
        }
      ],
      "image": {
        "type": "cartoon",
        "src": "img/cartoons/dimpled-ball.svg"
      },
      "author": "tom",
      "body": [
        "The model suggests a dimpled ball would meet less drag and keep more of its speed as it crosses the table. D'Alonzo stresses that his drag figures are illustrative rather than measured.",
        "The bigger unknowns are spin and bounce. D'Alonzo concludes that neither can be predicted reliably without making and testing real prototypes."
      ],
      "published": "2026-10-26T15:27:00Z"
    }
